### 5.2 Same-Author Prose Contrast Within `ElysiumAPI` & `AsphodelGradle`

* **Handwritten `CODE_CONVENTION.md` (`ElysiumAPI` Commit `ee37983ca3`, `2026-10-26`, no Oxford comma, comma splices):**
  ```text
  Hello, hello! If you're reading this then it probably means you want to contribute to Elysium API,
  if that's the case then this document will catch you up to speed on our various domain-specific conventions.
  ... right now our scope and targets are for the versions: `1.20.1` and `1.21.1` and the loaders: `fabric`, `forge` and `neoforge`.
  ```
* **`ElysiumAttachments.java` (`ElysiumAPI` Commit `3300787e5a`, `2026-11-07`, `5` consecutive Oxford commas + `<p>`/`{@code}`/`{@link}`):**
  ```java
  /**
   * The central facade for all data attachment operations in Elysium API.
   * <p>
   * This class provides a unified, cross-loader, and cross-version entry point
   * for reading and writing attachments on entities, block entities, and level
   * chunks. Whether you are running on 1.20.1 or 1.21.1, and regardless of
   * whether the underlying mod loader is Fabric, Forge, or NeoForge, the methods
   * here behave identically.
   * <p>
   * To register your attachment types, call {@link #register(String, Consumer)}
   * from your mod's common initialization phase. Once registered, use the various
   * {@code getData}, {@code setData}, {@code hasData}, and {@code removeData}
   * methods to work with attachment values on live holders.
   */
  ```
* **Intra-Commit Paragraph Alternation (`AsphodelGradle/README.md`, Commit `a0a06b49658573bf2660a638c8d62df8b6ebe8e8`, `2026-11-01`):**
  ```text
  [Line 195 - Opening paragraph]:
  `asphodel::mixin` is a resources-side directive made purely for convenience and utility sake. this one rewrites your mixin JSON config so that a mixin class gets added to the right array without you having to keep the config in sync by hand, which vastly reduces boilerplate in an enviornment like asphodel where you must curate delegate mixins carefull across the differing source sets.

  [Lines 207-214 - Immediately following paragraphs in same commit]:
  Conditions work exactly like the other directives, including the comparison operators. The directive must be placed above the `@Mixin` annotation on the class; anywhere else and the build warns and ignores it.

  A few things will get you a warning instead of an addition: a class without an `@Mixin` annotation, a directive placed below the annotation, a `file=` that escapes the resources root, a class outside the mixin config's `package`, and an entry that is already present in the array.
  ```
